== bonsai_fleet selftest (offline; no Vulkan object is created by any path below) ==

  -- ring algebra (no driver) --
  ok  : depth 4 x 16 KiB host ring allocates
  ok  : mask = depth-1, so the slot select is an AND and never a division
  ok  : total = depth x slot_bytes
  ok  : depth 3 refused - a non-power-of-two ring needs modulo in the per-boundary path
  ok  : depth 1 refused - it cannot hold a payload while the next one is produced
  ok  : slot_bytes 0 refused
  ok  : consecutive sequences use different slots
  ok  : sequence 4 wraps onto sequence 0's slot - the exact reason reserve() must refuse rather than evict
  ok  : an empty ring accepts seq 0
  ok  : publish is monotone: 4 publishes => produced 4
  ok  : readiness follows the cursor, not the wall clock
  ok  : a FULL ring refuses seq 4 instead of overwriting slot 0
  ok  : after one consume the wrapped sequence is admitted
  ok  : a slot's first and last word are addressable through the mapping at the wrapped index too

  -- split arithmetic (no driver) --
  ok  : 8 uniform layers over 2 cards inside an 8 MiB budget
  ok  : one contiguous range per card
  ok  : the ranges tile [0,8) with no gap and no overlap - the contiguity the GDN-state-never-crosses argument depends on
  ok  : byte conservation: sum(ranges) == layers + head + tail
  ok  : head charged to card 0, tail to the last card
  ok  : 2 ranges => 1 boundary crossing per token
  ok  : 1 card: everything on it, zero hops - the schedule degenerates to today's build
  ok  : cards > layers is refused instead of building an empty range
  ok  : a budget below (layers+head+tail)/cards is refused
  ok  : a head tensor larger than one card's budget is refused BEFORE any layer is assigned
  ok  : a byte table that does not cover every layer is refused
  ok  : uneven layer bytes still fit both budgets

  -- arena memory-type decisions (SYNTHETIC tables, no driver) --
  ok  : zero-copy REFUSES this table (no type is DEVICE_LOCAL|HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT at once) - the branch cannot fall back and the refusal is the answer, not a failure
  ok  : discrete branch picks the FIRST type carrying DEVICE_LOCAL and no other requirement - that is bonsai_vk.cpp:331-335's predicate, and ~30 existing logs are computed against exactly this index
  ok  : a discrete card's DEVICE_LOCAL heap is not reported as unified, so the ring import must not be skipped on its account
  ok  : zero-copy picks a DEVICE_LOCAL|HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT type
  ok  : and prefers the HOST_CACHED one - MEASURED, WC vs cached mapping is 24.8 ms against 6.28 ms over the same bytes (END_TO_END_MEASURED §2.2)
  ok  : and reports the heap unified, which is what makes the ring alias and the hop a store instead of a copy
  ok  : on the same table the discrete branch still returns the first DEVICE_LOCAL type: the MODE flag, not the device class, decides which branch runs
  ok  : DEVICE_LOCAL|HOST_VISIBLE without HOST_COHERENT is refused - a non-coherent mapping needs flushes the arena writer does not issue, so it would corrupt weights silently
  ok  : a DEVICE_LOCAL|HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT type on its own heap is still accepted (BAR-streaming weights are CPU-writable)
  ok  : but it is NOT called unified - so the ring-alias shortcut is not taken on a machine that only has BAR memory
  ok  : a single-type heap is not enough to call it unified

