================ ring 4 8 6 ================

== fleet ring rig: depth 4 x 8 MiB slots, 6 microbatch(es) ==
  card [0] Radeon RX 550X (DISCRETE_GPU) | compute family 0 | transfer-only family 2
  arena type 0  DEVICE_LOCAL   24.0 MiB  [shared-heap=no cached=no]  <- discrete branch: first type carrying DEVICE_LOCAL, exactly the predicate every existing runs/ log was produced with
  ring: 4 slot(s) x 8388608 B = 33554432 B, memory type 3 (host-visible, not on a unified heap)
  pipelined run below moves each 8388608 B slot D2H out of PROD and H2D into CONS, so the
  bytes cross the link twice per hop. NOT EXERCISABLE HERE: only one compute-capable device was opened, so the second card's H2D->compute overlap and the PCIe-to-PCIe path a two-card machine takes are untested, and the D2H->host->H2D round trip is what IS tested.

  pipelined run: 12 stage(s), 6 hop(s) in 19.028 ms
  per hop: wall 3171.3 us | host-visible waits: D2H fence 2706.7 us, doorbell stamp 0.3 us, back-pressure 3.2 us total over 2 wait(s), command-buffer recycle 17.8 us total over 8 wait(s)
  completion signal audit: fence column 2706.7 us/hop vs doorbell column 0.3 us/hop, 6 stamped / 0 timeout(s) -> fence and stamp agree (within 4x): with flags = 0 the fence armed by its own submit is a sound completion signal, and the stamp is kept as the assumption-free proof
  bytes across the ring: 50331648 B total, 8.00 MiB per hop; at the MEASURED 7.12 GB/s a 8388608 B slot is 1178.18 us of engine time per leg. Compare that with the doorbell column above: the hop is DMA-bound, not SYNC-bound - the earlier "sync-dominated" reading of this line was an artefact of timing the created-SIGNALED fence, which returns before the copy retires (MEASURED submit+fence round trip 99.4 us)
  back-pressure events: 2 at depth 4 (a depth-1 ring would take one wait per hop; D>1 is the mechanism, not a tuning knob)
  ring cursors: produced 6, consumed 6 (equal, as they must be after the drain)
  cursor cross-check: produced 6 vs hops 6 -> consistent
  D2H arrival: 12 word(s) read through the ring mapping over 4 written slot(s) (0 untouched), 0 mismatch(es) -> PASS - the DMA put into the slot exactly what the producer's dispatch wrote into its window, and the publish happened after the fence
  round trip slot1 -> CONS -> slot2: bytes intact (fence 1207.5 / 1349.6 us, + queue-idle 0.3 / 0.4 us; each leg is copy + one submit+fence round trip, MEASURED 99.4 us)
  fenced D2H audit: the fence alone reported 1204.1 us per hop, the queue-idle tail after it added 0.4 us (a tail near zero means the fence IS the completion signal here)
  fenced D2H  8 x 8388608 B on family 2: 1204.6 us per hop -> 6.96 GB/s wall = 97% of the MEASURED 7.15 GB/s ceiling, submit, fence AND the queue-drain tail included
  created-SIGNALED control 4 x 8388608 B: fence 0.8 us, queue-idle tail 1195.2 us -> REPRODUCED: the wait returned while the queue still had the copy in it. A fence created SIGNALED is already signalled when the wait runs, so it witnesses nothing; every fence in this repo is therefore created with flags = 0 and is armed only by its own submit.
  batched D2H 8 x 8388608 B in ONE submit: fence 9410.9 us total, queue-idle tail 0.6 us total -> 1176.4 us per copy, 7.13 GB/s end-to-end (MEASURED single-copy ceiling 7.15 GB/s; if the fence column alone exceeds it, the fence is not the completion signal and the SUM is the number to quote)

  publish condition in force: the DOORBELL stamp, written in the same CB as the copy, plus the fence; every fence here is created with flags = 0 and armed only by its own submit (a fence created SIGNALED is REPRODUCIBLY SATISFIED BEFORE ITS SUBMIT RETIRES on this driver, see the control above)
  device released. Whole command took 129 ms of GPU-touching time.
